rical polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nr=3,\\,(\\theta ,z)\\in C,\n$$\n\nwhere\n$C=[0,2\\pi )\\times [0,3].$\n\nHence, the cost to manufacture its mesh is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{∬}_{C}(1+\\frac{\\theta }{\\pi }+2z)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of double integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n{∬}_{C}(1+\\frac{\\theta }{\\pi }+2z)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{C}\\text{d}A+{∬}_{C}\\frac{\\theta }{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{C}2z\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is constant, so we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{C}\\text{d}A=\\text{Area}(C)=2\\pi \\cdot 3=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $z.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C}\\frac{\\theta }{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}A & =\\frac{(3-0)}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{\\theta }^{2}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\cdot {[{\\theta }^{2}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\pi }((2\\pi )^{2}-0) \\\\  & =6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the third integral is independent of $\\theta .$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{C}2z\\,\\text{d}A & =(2\\pi -0){\\int }_{0}^{3}2z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {[{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi ({3}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{∬}_{C}(1+\\frac{\\theta }{\\pi }+2z)\\,\\text{d}A=\\frac{1}{2\\pi }(6\\pi +6\\pi +18\\pi )=15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cost to manufacture the mesh of the cylindrical heater is $$15.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362491 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362490 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362490 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362490 1079 #edb/ref 17592186362491 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362490 1080 #uuid "08b51766-5aec-52ac-81b0-b241e0d6ebbd" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true]