                         \n                                        \n                                        ." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362555 1072 #uuid "e4caae42-4f39-5962-a56f-b059218d34d2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362555 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $r$, treating $\\theta $ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }[{\\int }_{0}^{2}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr]d\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{[-\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]}_{r=0}^{r=2}\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(-\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+0)\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,d\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,d\\theta  & =12[\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}]_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =6(1+1) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362556 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362557 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362556 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362556 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362556 1079 #edb/ref 17592186362557 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362556 1080 #uuid "b6ff5125-8cd4-53d2-a652-f69bb4a9afff" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362557 1081 "12" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362557 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362557 1084 #uuid "07b380bb-bc11-566f-9bb0-0e2a0179712b" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362559 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362558 1069 "q-169206" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362558 1071 "![](images/cf/cfbc570ba4fc6bd4923edc2d0efae0e735e671f9b4350226159e591e0bdae6c7.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The net $L$ is represented by the line segment shown in the diagram above. \nCalculate the flux of $F$ through $L,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{L}^{}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362558 1072 #uuid "c4eeab3d-e63c-5f1b-81ca-c4c4901eb313" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362558 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n* the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n* the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the line segment $L,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}t,\\,y=-\\sqrt{2}t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the line from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =3x{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}t)(-\\sqrt{2}t)^{2} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-{y}^{3} \\\\  & =-(-\\sqrt{2}t)^{3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}t)=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{2}t)=-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{L}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{L}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3})\\cdot (-\\sqrt{2})-(2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3})\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-12{t}^{3}-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-16{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4[{t}^{4}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4(1-0) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362559 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362560 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362559 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362559 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362559 1079 #edb/ref 17592186362560 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362559 1080 #uuid "674f2487-9757-5196-815f-fe7a2e6b0fcb" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362560 1081 "-4" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362560 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362560 1084 #uuid "d3de62fa-1c74-52d8-bd89-6f7ea249f514" 13