's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nAs we saw earlier, we can use Green's theorem to write our line integral in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{∂R}3x{y}^{2}\\,dx-{y}^{3}\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nBut this time, our region $R$ (in polar coordinates) is given by \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-3{[\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-2}]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{r=1}^{r=2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362562 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362563 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362562 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362562 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362562 1079 #edb/ref 17592186362563 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362562 1080 #uuid "53b1b3ff-21b1-5213-8b9c-584dd6a1a1eb" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362563 1081 "0" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362563 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362563 1084 #uuid "ff3661cf-178f-52bd-85c7-48d75d20b100" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362564 1058 #edb/ref 17592186362574 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362564 1062 #uuid "00988180-3b53-5fa2-9094-97a99ad88ec2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362564 1063 #edb/ref 17592186362533 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186362565 1058 #edb/ref 1759